+  "title": "GOV-0061: BADF-WP-GIT-H — release: `git-release-check` verifies a release ref is annotated, on main, unmoved and record-bound (TAG_EXISTS != RELEASE_AUTHORIZED); `git-release-record` writes the binding, never a tag; the baseline pinned; release-versioning subskill",
+  "problem": "## Observed\n\n`badf-git`'s release contract (`references/release-versioning.md`) binds a release to an authorized, verified `main` revision, its result-tree identity, a version/tag identity and release evidence, and states three inequalities — `MERGED != RELEASED`, **`TAG_EXISTS != RELEASE_AUTHORIZED`**, `RELEASED != PRODUCTION_VERIFIED`. It requires the version to come from an explicit release record or approved version source (never a branch name, a PR title, a commit prefix, or \"a tag that an unauthorized actor can create\"), release refs to be created only from `main`, and published release/baseline refs to be **immutable** — never moved, deleted, recreated or reused. Measured on `main` after GIT-G: the repository has exactly one release-class ref, the annotated, **unsigned** `BADF-BASELINE-1.0.0` → `3f6119b` (the unmodified import), no `vX.Y.Z` tag, no GitHub release, no version source of truth anywhere (`badf/lifecycle.json` carries only `policy_epoch`), no tag ruleset (the one ruleset targets branches), and **nothing that checks a tag** — `badf_gate.py` contains no tag logic at all. The baseline tag is already load-bearing (the G00 example dossier's `source_revision` is that tag): if it were moved, the example would silently govern a different commit. GTGM v0.1 §14 (retain `BADF-BASELINE-X.Y.Z`, introduce `vX.Y.Z`; SemVer as a governance decision; protected immutable tags with release evidence), §15 (`release-versioning` subskill), §17 **GIT-H — Release**.\n\n## Expected\n\n**A release ref becomes a checked binding; creating one stays a human act.**\n\n1. **`badf_gate.py git-release-check <tag> [<path>]`** (read-only, `GIT-O0`) verifies a release-class ref against the contract: the tag exists and is **annotated** (a lightweight tag is refused — it carries no tagger identity); its commit is on **`main`'s first-parent history** (release-from-main; a topic commit is refused); a **release record** exists at `badf/releases/<tag>.json` and binds this exact `source_revision` **and** `source_result_tree` (a record for a different commit means the tag *moved* — refused as an immutability breach; no record means `TAG_EXISTS != RELEASE_AUTHORIZED` — `BLOCKED`); the version in the record equals the tag name; and the record's provenance statement matches the tag (`signed: false` recorded, not hidden). Renders one JSON verdict (`RELEASE_BOUND` / `BLOCKED`) with the contract's `release_binding` fields observed from the object store; deterministic, no network.\n2. **`badf_gate.py git-release-record <version> [<path>]`** (`GIT-O1`: writes one file, never a ref) writes `badf/releases/<version>.json` — `version`, `tag_ref`, `source_ref: refs/heads/<default>`, `source_revision`, `source_result_tree`, `content_tree` (the GIT-E construction, for continuity with composition records), `policy_epoch`, `provenance: {annotated, signed, tagger}` when the tag already exists, `release_authority: HUMAN_REQUIRED` and `disposition: HUMAN_REQUIRED` — a **request**, exactly like the self-dossier. It refuses when HEAD is not a first-parent commit of `origin/<default>` (release-from-main), when a record for that version already exists with a different revision (reuse), or when the version is not `vX.Y.Z` / `BADF-BASELINE-X.Y.Z`. **It never runs `git tag`**: the tag is created by the release authority with `git tag -a`/`gh release` after the record, and `git-release-check` then verifies the pair.\n3. **The historical baseline is recorded and pinned**: `badf/releases/BADF-BASELINE-1.0.0.json` binds `3f6119b` and its tree with `provenance: {annotated: true, signed: false}` and the contract's note (\"historical; provenance limitation recorded, not rewritten\"); `badf_gate.py repo` gains the **immutability guard** — every record under `badf/releases/` whose tag exists must still point at the recorded revision, else `repo` fails (`release ref moved`). A tag that is recorded but absent locally is reported, not refused (a shallow clone is not a breach).\n4. **`release-versioning` subskill** (GTGM §15): the SemVer decision contract as the release authority's checklist (MAJOR/MINOR/PATCH criteria from the frozen reference; a decision, never a commit-prefix inference), the record → tag → check sequence, the two tag families and what each means, and the rule that a bad release is superseded or revoked by a new record, never by moving a tag. Registered `IMPLEMENTED`, C1, `allowed_tools: []`; the `badf-git` root stays `DESIGNED`.\n5. **CI positive control:** the runner runs `git-release-check BADF-BASELINE-1.0.0` (tags are fetched with `fetch-depth: 0`) and must render `RELEASE_BOUND`.\n6. **Not decided here:** whether and when BADF cuts its first `vX.Y.Z` — a release-authority decision (`HUMAN_REQUIRED`), enabled by this WP, not taken by it.\n\n## Acceptance criteria\n\n1. `git-release-check BADF-BASELINE-1.0.0` → `RELEASE_BOUND`, `source_revision 3f6119b…`, `provenance.signed: false` recorded, exit 0 — locally and on the runner.\n2. A lightweight tag → refused (`annotated`); a tag on a topic commit not on `main`'s first-parent → refused (release-from-main); a tag with no record → `BLOCKED` naming `TAG_EXISTS != RELEASE_AUTHORIZED`; a record whose revision differs from the tag's → refused as a moved tag; a record whose version ≠ tag → refused.\n3. `git-release-record v0.1.0` on a first-parent `main` commit writes the binding with all fields and `disposition: HUMAN_REQUIRED`; on a topic commit → refused; for a version already recorded against a different revision → refused; an unsupported version form → refused; it never creates a tag (`git tag -l` unchanged).\n4. `repo` refuses when a recorded release tag points elsewhere (moved), and passes when the tag is absent locally (reported).\n5. Read-only proven for `git-release-check` (refs/index/status byte-identical); deterministic modulo `observed_at`; each refusal and guard branch **load-bearing** (mutation).\n6. `release-versioning` registered `IMPLEMENTED` C1 `allowed_tools: []` with a real digest; root stays `DESIGNED`; no new script, no schema (records are artifacts; `badf/releases/` is a new governed directory covered by the lockfile), no ruleset/authority change; `repo` clean; full suite + compose green in CI.\n\n## Tests (`tests/test_badf_git_release.py`, scratch clones via `tests/_scratch.seed_clone`; tags created locally with `git tag -a`)\n\n- `test_baseline_tag_is_RELEASE_BOUND`\n- `test_lightweight_tag_is_refused`\n- `test_tag_off_main_first_parent_is_refused`\n- `test_tag_without_record_is_BLOCKED_tag_exists_is_not_authorized`\n- `test_moved_tag_is_refused_as_immutability_breach`\n- `test_record_version_must_equal_tag`\n- `test_release_record_writes_the_binding_as_HUMAN_REQUIRED_and_never_tags`\n- `test_release_record_refuses_off_main_reuse_and_bad_version_forms`\n- `test_repo_guard_refuses_a_moved_recorded_tag_and_tolerates_an_absent_one`\n- `test_release_check_is_read_only_and_deterministic`\n- `test_release_versioning_subskill_registered_and_root_stays_designed`\n\n## Out of scope\n\n- Creating, moving, deleting or signing any tag; creating a GitHub release; tag rulesets or signature enforcement (platform policy — recorded as a provenance limitation, applied to future refs by a later decision).\n- Cutting the first `vX.Y.Z` (release-authority decision); SBOM/attestation/deployment records (G10–G13 evidence types, measured gates); the remote-workspace mode (#133).\n- A version bump ledger or CHANGELOG (a later decision, if BADF ever publishes artifacts).\n\n## Risk / authority / stop conditions\n\n- Change class **C1** (read-only check; one artifact file written on request; no ref mutation; no authority change). Target gate **G07**, `HUMAN_REQUIRED`.\n- Stop if the guard would require network, a tag ruleset, or signing; or if scope grows past two subcommand modes + one `repo` guard + one record + one subskill + tests + doctrine.\n- Lean mode: **enforced** — reuse `_git_at`, `content_tree`, `ledger_landings`' first-parent walk, the scratch fixture.\n\n## Identity\n\n- Ledger **WP-2026-0081** · demand **BADF-DEM-0068** · this issue **GOV-0061** · phase label BADF-WP-GIT-H. Ids measured across `main`, every remote branch head and all issues at post time.\n- Branch `wp/WP-2026-0081-git-release` in a linked worktree from current `main`; reconciles whatever is `LANDED_UNRECONCILED` at branch time.\n- Sequence: A ✓ B ✓ C ✓ D ✓ E ✓ F ✓ G ✓ → **H (this)** → I → J.\n\nsource: GTGM v0.1 §14, §15, §17 (GIT-H); `release-versioning.md`; `docs/11-release-production.md` · discovered-by: BARCHI-2, the untested load-bearing baseline tag and the absent version source · status: PROPOSED\n\n🧠 **BARCHI-2** — plan posted; engineered under the operator's `/loop your goal on badf-git`.\n<!-- agent: architect -->\n",
